 If the \fInewLength\fR argument is less than the current length
 of the value's string, with \fIobjPtr->length\fR is reduced without
 reallocating the string space; the original allocated size for the
 string is recorded in the value, so that the string length can be
 enlarged in a subsequent call to \fBTcl_SetObjLength\fR without
-reallocating storage.  In all cases \fBTcl_SetObjLength\fR leaves
-a null character at \fIobjPtr->bytes[newLength]\fR.
+reallocating storage.  If the value has a string representation,
+\fBTcl_SetObjLength\fR leaves a null character at
+\fIobjPtr->bytes[newLength]\fR.
+.PP
+If the value has no string representation (\fIobjPtr->bytes\fR is NULL)
+but has an internal Unicode representation, for example a value created
+by \fBTcl_NewUnicodeObj\fR, then \fBTcl_SetObjLength\fR instead changes
+the length of the Unicode representation: \fInewLength\fR is taken as a
+number of characters, not bytes, and \fIobjPtr->bytes\fR stays NULL.
+Callers that need direct access to a byte buffer of \fInewLength\fR
+bytes should call \fBTcl_GetString\fR on the value first.
 .PP
